Define the complete semantic contract for component `multiplier` before its VHDL is written. Return Markdown only. This contract will be normative for the VHDL implementation, Cocotb tests, and dependent components. Do not infer or mention implementation internals. State timing, reset behavior (or `none`), all input/output semantics, every opcode/selector encoding when applicable, and boundary behavior. Use exactly these level-2 headings: `## Timing`, `## Reset`, `## Behavior`, `## Operations`, `## Edge cases`, and `## Test strategy`. `## Operations` may say `none` only when there is no encoded selector.

The test strategy is normative and must be an executable evidence plan, not generic coverage advice. For each public behavior that selects, indexes, routes, packs, slices, extends, decodes, or transforms data: identify control classes, state the causal input-to-output relation, require distinguishable source values where a wrong selection could otherwise pass, and vary one semantic factor at a time. Do not mention implementation internals.

Responsibility: Compute the low or high 32 bits of the 64-bit product with signed/signed, signed/unsigned or unsigned/unsigned interpretation, selected by a 2-bit operation code (00 MUL, 01 MULH, 10 MULHSU, 11 MULHU, equal to funct3[1:0]).
Fixed decomposition (approved architecture): `multiplier` has no generated dependencies.
Public interface:
```vhdl
entity multiplier is
    port(
        operand_a : in std_logic_vector(31 downto 0);
        operand_b : in std_logic_vector(31 downto 0);
        mul_op : in std_logic_vector(1 downto 0);
        result : out std_logic_vector(31 downto 0)
    );
end multiplier;
```

